  "query_preview": "..\n\nProgressive shortwave ridging will carry the center of the surface \nanticyclone out of Southeast Michigan to over the Mid Atlantic \nstates by this evening. Strength and positioning of the surface high \nwill result in the development of a notable southwest gradient wind \nwith gusts later today of approximately 15 to 25 mph, highest speeds \nnorth of I 69 particularly near Saginaw Bay. Not surprisingly the \nforecast soundings show good mixing through 5.0 kft agl with highs \ntoday in the middle to upper 60s. Persistent gradient tonight yields \nwarmer overnight temperatures with lows in the 50s areawide.\n\nExit region to next Pacific upper level jet packet will support \ntroughing into the western and northern Great Lakes Wednesday. Very \ngood model signal for the development of a cold front with a little \nwarm thetae surge in advance of the feature here Wednesday. \nConvective allowing models suggest rain shower potential tonight in \nthe moisture transport corridor especially north of Metro Detroit. \nForecast models suggest that perhaps best case 500 J/kg MUCAPE will \nbe possible. The real problem and limiting factor will be the amount \nof stability that is projected to remain in the 6.0 to 14.0 kft agl \nlayer. Will be an interesting all or nothing scenario perhaps for a \nshower or a rumble of thunder, with some favorable cloud top cooling \nprocesses likely required to help steepen midlevel lapse rates. The \nforecast currently resides dry for tonight. Warm and mild Wednesday \nafternoon with temperatures reaching the lower to middle 70s. A good \nlittle setup for some overachievement. Rapid expansion of the \nwavelength of the troughing may cause a disjointed frontal structure \nwith the NAM suggesting active downward vertical motion through the \n800-900mb layer already by 19-20z. This lower level subsidence \ncombined with very stable midlevel limits any potential for \nmeasurable precipitation later Wednesday.\n\nMoisture lean midlevels and a stable midlevel carries out dry \nweather through the end of the week. Near to below normal \ntemperatures are expected Thursday and Friday with highs in the 60s. \nVery impressive ridging continues to be advertised for the weekend \nand beginning of next week. Could be looking at a possibility for \ntemperatures to make a run at 80 degrees for Sunday and Monday \ndependent on cloud. One item to watch for will be the possiblity of \nGulf moisture to make a direct run at Southeast Michigan late \nSunday. If that were to develop and clip Southeast Michigan there \nwould be high rainfall rates based on climatology.",
